first of all welcome everybody uh i am  very pleased to be here to  talk about this program and i'll also  specifically tell you about  uh you know the drug discovery and the  development aspects that we will be  talking about  uh from what i just heard from sujit  this seems like a very exciting program  uh from a person who is willing to learn  about the  nuances of drug discovery i see that  there's a beautiful blend of theoretical  as well as practical knowledge that you  will gain  so i i really commend uh omics  for putting together such a beautiful  program  let me introduce myself first  so my name is uday saxena and as  sujeet said i am co-founder of a startup  biotech company called  reagene biosciences  i have a phd and a postdoc  like a lot of people who are in research  in  cell molecular biology and pharmacology  i started my career in a drug  industry in a company called park davis  pfizer in the u.s  and i was part of the team that  discovered a drug called lipitor  uh some of you might know it as an  autostatin  the brand name is lipitor this is a drug  that lowers  uh total body cholesterol and is used to  prevent heart diseases  uh the interesting thing about this  particular drug that i  i really am excited to uh tell you about  is the fact that this was the largest  selling  drug to date that has been known  uh to humans so the peak sales of their  drug were between 17 to 20  billion dollars a year so it was really  uh  what in our industry we call a huge  blockbuster  from there on i went to do a startup  company in the u.s called  atherogenics where i was head of r d  and we took an idea all the way  into clinical trials i then became the  chief scientific  officer for dr reddy's which as you or  some of you may know is one of the  largest  indian pharma companies based out of  hyderabad  and after that i have become an  entrepreneur and  doing a startup company myself  so so that's about myself i am part of  an organization  called faba and faba is a  not-for-profit organization that brings  together  it's called the federation of asian  biotechnology associations the whole  idea is to bring together  uh different biotechnology bodies from  india as well as enabling neighboring  asian countries and share knowledge  and faba academy which is actually a  it's it's something that has been  launched by faba to educate young people  in the nuances of drug discovery so  we are currently in partnership with  omics right now and very excited to be  able to participate in this  let me tell you how drug discovery works  and also want to tell you what's the  benefit of some  for people like you to be going through  this course so  first of all uh i don't know how many of  you know this  drug discovery is an extremely long  process  it takes an average of 12 to 15 years  from having an  idea to all the way to bringing a drug  to the market  so it's a very long process the other  thing about  drug discovery is that it's very  expensive  the current estimates of bringing a new  drug to the market  is about a billion to two billion  dollars that's how much money  the company a pharma company has to  spend  before they can successfully launch a  drug  on the market so not only is it time  consuming  it's also a very expensive process so  why am i telling you this the reason i'm  telling you  this is the more knowledge we can have  about the idea that you want to launch a  drug on  the more likely it is that you're going  to be successful  most of the drugs are based around  protein targets and you will learn a lot  about protein targets in the program  that's been  put together and so it the idea would  start from saying  okay this is a protein that's involved  in say alzheimer's  and we need to block its activity say if  it's an enzyme  so up front you need to know everything  about that protein  enzyme its crystal structure its docking  you know the whole nine yards so the  more information  you have about your target protein  the more likely it is that you're going  to be successful  in using that to launch a product so the  reason i'm telling you and emphasizing  that  is this program appears to have all  elements that are needed to successfully  choose a target validate a target  understand how it works and then design  drugs around it so  this program really to to my knowledge  is one of the more extensive  programs i've seen that will educate you  on right from the beginning of drug  discovery which is how do you choose a  protein target  right so you'll learn that once you  learn that you have to design  drugs against it and you will also learn  some of that in this  uh suji talked about a hit to a lead  so a hit is nothing but your initial  drug candidates that you have and then  off the thousand that you may have you  have to  then whittle it down to maybe five or  ten molecules  that you choose forward so from a hit of  thousand you go to lead which is five  how do you do that there's actually a  very very well thought out process that  we go through in the industry to do that  and in this program you're going to  learn about that as well  hit to lead and sujit has already talked  about that  then the drug moves into animal proof of  concept features you have to demonstrate  if it is alzheimer's you have to  recreate alzheimer's in an animal model  and show your lead candidates actually  provide any therapeutic benefits so  you'll learn some of that as well in  najeet kamats lecture  you learn about protein validation and  how drug discovery is taken from  selecting a target all the way to  establishing proof of concept  the final and the most expensive part of  drug discovery it's what is known as  clinical trials  clinical trials are important because  the drugs we design  are eventually consumed by human beings  so there are two things that regulatory  bodies such as the united states food  and drug  administration wants from you  if you are a former company you have to  demonstrate to them  that the drug that you have designed is  not only going to be therapeutic which  means it's efficacious  and protect against alzheimer's but it  also has to be  safe it's like a pilot right a pilot not  only has to learn how to fly a plane but  it has to be done  safely the reason that is important is  because  a lot of human lives are our state  similarly  in the drug discovery and development  process  millions of human lives are at stake so  the us fda  and other regulatory bodies in the world  want to make sure that the drug that you  want approved and launched in the market  is safe in order to demonstrate that  you have to take it through clinical  trials  and the clinical trials typically start  in what is known as  phase one clinical trials where you do  some healthy volunteers and demonstrate  that it is safe  then you move into a small alzheimer's  population maybe  500 alzheimer's population and show that  it is efficacious  and then you have to move it into like a  5 000 alzheimer's patients  that is called phase 3 to show that it  is not just  safe but also active in a larger  population  so this is what is known as taking a  phase one all the way through an  nda once you complete all this  you assimilate all that information into  a document  called new drug application or nda  you submit that to the fda the fda will  probably take six months to a year  to very carefully review it and then  say yes the drug is approved or not  that's really the end  so you will also and i will be talking  uh to you about that and i will also not  just talk about how to do  a phase one phase two phase three but  how do you successfully design a  clinical trial right  because clinical trials alone take about  70 percent of the budget  so if you're going to spend half a  billion dollars clinical trials probably  will take up two to three hundred  million dollars of that  so the whole idea is not to fail in a  clinical trial  all of the failures if you want to have  any should happen  in the beginning part and that's what  you will learn how do you select a  target  how do you validate it how do you design  a drug it is okay to fail at that stage  because it's not very expensive  but as a company we do not like to fail  in a later stages because it's so much  more expensive  right so i will tell you how certain  drugs were  successfully designed for example i will  tell you about a torostatin lipitor  it was actually the seventh product in  its class  being launched and yet it became the  front runner  why was that what was it about that drug  that made it competitively beat out six  other similar drugs and went ahead what  was the strategy what was the rationale  what was the clinical trial it's very  important that the clinical trial has to  be designed  from a market perspective also it's it's  not just enough to be able to  demonstrate that your drug is safe and  active  but you also have to convince a  physician  to prescribe it you know the physician  will want to know  why should i prescribe your drug versus  the three other drug that's already  there  right and that's all science driven  that's the science that goes behind the  commercialization so  i will spend a lot of time behind how  successful drugs were designed and i  will also showcase for you  how a bad clinical trial design  and i've gone through that also not only  have i gone through a successful drug  design  but in the startup company that i went  to after park davis  we actually had a drug fail in phase  three after spending 800 million dollars  so what went wrong what were the lessons  so i'll tell you about that as well  so um i think in summary what i'm trying  to tell you is that this is a very  exciting course for anybody who wants to  be educated  in this whole business of translational  research  slash drug discovery and as somebody who  has run large organizations such as dr  reddy's  i'll tell you one of the things i looked  for when i was hiring people was  what knowledge do they have of the drug  discovery business that they get into  right  so more likely i always like to  hire people that had more knowledge  about  what they were getting into than people  who had no knowledge at all so  some of you may be interested in a  career in the industry  so this course will educate you and what  it takes to  get to the industry so i'm very excited  to be part of uh this program  and really look forward to meeting all  of you  uh i believe my session is on the 22nd  of july  but you will also hear from some of my  colleagues sujeeth manson ajith kamat  ajit is also an industry veteran he has  worked at pfizer  and he's going to tell you about the  early phase you know how do you select a  drug and all that  so both parts are very important what's  called  the preclinical part that ajit is going  to address  and the clinical part which is what i'm  going to address so  i think i'm going to stop now but thank  you very much for your time and i hope  to and i hope and look forward to seeing  you  at these workshops
 

